WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.960
Film Courage: Why did it take years for this
film [THE VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL] to be

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:03.960
made?

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.759
Shawn Christensen, co-screenwriter/director:
We had the film optioned and then bought out

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:13.770
by a really great independent producer and
a great director.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:22.460
And then they couldn’t get the money together
or something and we were just kind of poor

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.730
kids sitting around waiting for them to do
something.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.460
And then it just kind of…they bought it
out because they thought they were going to

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.269
make it and they didn’t and then it got
stuck at Fox.

00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:38.149
After awhile they all left Fox and Fox (the
studio itself) didn’t realize they even

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.930
had this screenplay.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:49.109
So it was a little loophole with the WGA (The
Writers Guild) about getting your screenplay

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:50.109
back.

00:00:50.109 --> 00:00:54.510
Most people (I should say) don’t get their
screenplays back but this particular screenplay,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:00.589
it’s a small script or it’s a small premise
and we thought we had a shot at buying it

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:04.309
back for the price we sold it for (which was
not very much).

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:11.280
And we asked Fox if they would give it back
to us and you know they did.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:18.990
They gave it back to us for basically the
price that we bought it for and we went and

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:19.990
made it.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:24.860
But we had to wait with that clause, with
that WGA clause, you have to wait 5 years.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.540
You have to wait 5 years after the last rewrite
or the last active…(it’s called Active

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:39.770
Development) and a studio will find any way
they can to not give you back your script.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.149
Active Development can be misconstrued as
anything.

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:44.340
I mean that could be…a lunch.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.770
I mean they could say anything.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.480
That’s not really illegal, it’s so loose,
you know?

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:58.450
But Fox, simply put they just didn’t know
that the screenplay was in their vault because

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.299
the people who had brought it in and left
and we got it back.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:06.470
Film Courage: Did you lay awake at night thinking
“I want this screenplay back” or it was

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.000
more a process where it came at the right
time (the five years was up)?

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.930
Shawn Christensen: It was more in the back
of my head.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:22.240
When I had heard about this WGA clause (the
five-year thing) which was like a couple months

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:30.500
after we had done a quick rewrite on it, I
kind of just set my internal back-of-my-head

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:38.880
timer and Jason Dolan (the co-screenwriter),
we just kind of set it in our heads that whenever

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:49.210
it was (2016 I think it was) we are able to
reacquire the screenplay and by that time

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:56.940
I think I had just made my first feature and
I was able to attract more interest to make

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.810
a second feature and so the timing was right
on it.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:06.880
But really to be truthful I actually didn’t
necessarily want to make the movie, I just

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.850
wanted the script back.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.900
But…we made it.

